+//! What opening a store does about the log a previous process left behind.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`, and the third of that step's three pieces: the bytes are the
+//! log's ([`crate::engine::wal::segment`]), the answer to "which of them proved to be records" is
+//! [`crate::engine::wal::replay`]'s, and this module is what the *store* does with that answer when
+//! it is opened — and what it tells whoever opened it.
+//!
+//! Opening is the only place a recovery can happen: a store that is already running holds offsets
+//! into the file, a session that has answered clients, and a flush boundary; there is no moment in
+//! the middle of that where "what the log proves" can change under it. So `Spool::with_policy`
+//! replays the log before it counts a single byte, and this is where the result is handed back.
+//!
+//! The signature below is this step's contract — the spec calls it, and the daemon reads it to say
+//! what a restart cost — and its body is the step's work.
+
+use crate::engine::wal::replay::Recovered;
+
+use super::Spool;
+
+impl Spool {
+    /// What opening this store had to do: the records replay proved, and the bytes it threw away.
+    ///
+    /// The operator's half of `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`, and the reason it is a value rather than a
+    /// log line written here: the engine does not own a log format or a level, and whoever opened
+    /// the store is the one who has to say what it found — the daemon does exactly that
+    /// (`bin/lakeletd.rs`). [`Recovered::discarded`] of zero means the log ended on a record
+    /// boundary, which is the shape a clean stop leaves; anything else is data a crash took, and it
+    /// is the caller's business to say so out loud.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn recovered(&self) -> &Recovered {
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery")
+    }
+}
