+//! Replaying a store: the records its log proves, and what had to be thrown away.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`. [`segment`] reads the bytes; this module is the answer the store
+//! and the operator need from them — *which* records survived and how much of the file was not one.
+//!
+//! One rule decides the whole walk, and it is the step's central sentence: **replay stops at the
+//! first thing it cannot prove.** Write the argument for it down here, because it is the design
+//! decision of the step and the one a reviewer will push on. The three costs, in the order they cost
+//! somebody, are worth naming in your own words:
+//!
+//! * a log is a **prefix** by construction, so "the record after a bad one" is an offset the bad
+//!   record's length field described — it is not a place in the file, it is a conclusion drawn from
+//!   bytes that have just been shown to be untrustworthy;
+//! * a **gap is worse than a short log**: a store that returns records 1–10, drops 11 and returns 12
+//!   has told every reader the series was continuous, and a wrong answer nobody can see is worse than
+//!   a smaller answer the counts report;
+//! * what follows a tear is **not data**, it is the middle of a record: the rest of that record's
+//!   payload, as much as the device committed.
+//!
+//! So the answer has three parts — the records, the bytes that were proved, and the bytes that were
+//! thrown away — and the store moves the log's end to the first of those numbers, so the next append
+//! continues there instead of writing after bytes nobody can read ([`truncate`], called by
+//! `Spool::with_policy`). That is what makes recovery something a store *does at open* rather than a
+//! report it prints: a tail left in place would stop every later replay at the same byte.
+//!
+//! What recovery deliberately does **not** do: it does not call [`Spool::sync`], it does not flush,
+//! and it does not repair a record. It reads what the device has. Putting bytes on the device is
+//! `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr`'s decision, and a reader that also wrote would be a reader nobody
+//! could run against a store they care about.
+//!
+//! [`Spool`]: crate::engine::Spool
+//! [`Spool::sync`]: crate::engine::Spool::sync
+
+use std::path::Path;
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+use super::segment::Stop;
+
+/// What replaying a store's log proved.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Recovered {
+    /// The records whose seal verified, in the order they were appended.
+    records: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
+    /// Bytes of the log that are proved: the header plus every whole record.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// Bytes that were not part of a proved record.
+    discarded: u64,
+    /// Why the replay stopped there.
+    stop: Stop,
+}
+
+impl Recovered {
+    /// The records recovery proved, in the order they were appended.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn records(&self) -> &[Vec<u8>] {
+        &self.records
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes of the log are proved: its header plus every whole record.
+    ///
+    /// The offset the store continues at — [`truncate`] cuts the file here, so the next append writes
+    /// after the last record this replay could verify and never over a byte it did.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes were **not** part of a proved record.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn discarded(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.discarded
+    }
+
+    /// Why the replay stopped where it did.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn stop(&self) -> Stop {
+        self.stop
+    }
+}
+
+/// Replay the log of the store in `directory`.
+///
+/// The file is the one `Spool` writes (`crate::engine::spool::FILE`), and a store that has never
+/// accepted anything — no file, or an empty one — answers "no records, nothing discarded", because a
+/// store this process has not written to yet is not a corrupt one. A file that is *not* this store's
+/// log is the other answer: [`Error::Protocol`], never an empty store; [`segment::scan`] is where that
+/// decision is made and its module docs are where the reason is.
+///
+/// Read-only: nothing here changes the file. [`truncate`] is the other half, and keeping the two apart
+/// is what lets a repair tool replay a copy of a store, or ask what a capture of bytes would become,
+/// without touching anything.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the file cannot be read, and [`Error::Protocol`] when what is in it is not
+/// this log.
+pub fn recover(directory: &Path) -> Result<Recovered, Error> {
+    let _ = directory;
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery")
+}
+
+/// Cut the log of the store in `directory` back to `bytes`, so the next append continues there.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the file cannot be opened or cannot be cut — a store whose tail cannot be
+/// removed cannot be appended to either, because writing behind bytes nobody can read would hide them
+/// in the middle of the log for good.
+pub fn truncate(directory: &Path, bytes: u64) -> Result<(), Error> {
+    let _ = (directory, bytes);
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery")
+}
